julia: Running into an edge-only failure in a Next.js edge function: 'TypeError: Request is not a constructor' when using our JS SDK with an undici polyfill. Anyone seen this combo before? Using ESM build of the SDK and Node 20 runtime.

sam: Yep, seen that. Edge runtimes already provide web fetch/Request/Headers. The issue comes when a polyfill or undici is forced into the global scope and clashes with the runtime's native constructors. Quick workaround is to avoid global polyfills in edge and do a conditional fallback. Example snippet that worked for me:

```js
// prefer native web fetch in edge, otherwise dynamically import undici for node
const getFetch = async () => {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function' && typeof globalThis.Request === 'function') {
    return globalThis.fetch;
  }
  const { fetch } = await import('undici');
  return fetch;
};

// usage in SDK init
const fetchImpl = await getFetch();
const res = await fetchImpl(url, opts);
```

kevin: Another angle: TS typing gets weird because undici's types can conflict with lib.dom. If folks see errors like 'Request type mismatch', try making your SDK's public types agnostic to undici specifics. Example: export the fetch type as a DOM-compatible signature so consumers on edge (lib: dom) and node (lib: dom, dom.iterable, es2021) both type check:

```ts
export type FetchFn = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>;
export type FetchLike = typeof globalThis.fetch | FetchFn;
```

Then accept a fetch option in the client constructor: `new Client({ fetch?: FetchLike })`. That avoids leaking undici/Node-specific types into the public surface.

alex: Also watch for CJS vs ESM differences — requiring undici in a CJS context or bundler can cause surprises. Always prefer dynamic import for runtime selection. For clarity in SDK code we used:

```js
let nodeFetch;
try {
  nodeFetch = (await import('undici')).fetch;
} catch (e) {
  // bundlers / edge will just use globalThis.fetch
  nodeFetch = undefined;
}
const fetchImpl = nodeFetch ?? globalThis.fetch;
```

sam: For DX/onboarding notes we should: 1) Document a one-liner to opt-out of the polyfill when using edge (e.g., "do not import undici/polyfill-global"). 2) Ship a tiny helper in the SDK (getPreferredFetch) that does the detection and returns a typed fetch. 3) Add an FAQ entry: "Edge runtime error: Request is not a constructor" with the snippet above and a link to a minimal sample app.

julia: Tried the dynamic import snippet — it fixed the runtime error in our edge function. Thanks! :tada: Will open a small PR to the sample-apps repo to show the recommended pattern.

